{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvykQ7ZVWGjDndGEfW9P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019fRUALrp3WBTGtjAZ2DR64","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; grep -c \"^@\" references.bib; grep -n \"cite\" paper.tex | head -40; find / -name figure_manifest.schema.json 2>/dev/null | head -2; grep -n \"selection manifest\\|frozen\\|once\" paper.tex | head -30"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5365,"cache_read_input_tokens":43651,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5365},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"529359a3-cd47-4a89-a29c-a6a024fe0bef","uuid":"6f9666b6-b27c-4f23-bde8-932deeeaf182","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:25:38.240Z","request_id":"req_011CgvykPU7iZB26gf3uSvJE"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019fRUALrp3WBTGtjAZ2DR64","type":"tool_result","content":"30\n40:Many predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibration set into prediction sets whose marginal coverage is at least $1-\\alpha$ under exchangeability, in finite samples and without distributional assumptions. In practice, however, three questions that matter to a user are not answered by the marginal guarantee: does coverage hold within classes or confidence strata, does it survive a change in the input distribution, and is it achieved with sets that are small enough to be informative? Our motivation is that a method tuned for one question may do worse on another, so reporting only average coverage and set size can hide such differences; conditional-coverage and efficiency work \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Kiyani2024LengthOI} makes the individual questions concrete.\n62:Split conformal prediction uses a held-out calibration set and retains finite-sample marginal coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic,Lei2018Distribution}; Mondrian conformal prediction applies the same rank argument within partition cells \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic}. For classifiers, the least-ambiguous-set construction thresholds the softmax mass of each label \\citep{Sadinle2019Least}, adaptive prediction sets accumulate sorted probability mass with randomisation \\citep{Romano2020Classification}, and a regularised variant penalises large ranks to shrink sets on many-class problems \\citep{Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty}. These are our comparators (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n65:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has been proposed for bias reduction \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-conditional coverage with many classes \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP}, conformal prediction for long-tailed classification \\citep{Ding2025ConformalPF}, and class-wise coverage via augmented label rank calibration \\citep{Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n68:Set size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Conformal methods have also been combined with Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n71:Conformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging and histopathology \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF}. We describe these works only by title and venue.\n74:Under covariate shift, exchangeability fails. If the density ratio is known, reweighting the calibration scores by it and giving the test point its own weight restores marginal coverage \\citep{Tibshirani2019Conformal}; estimating the ratio with a calibration-versus-test classifier makes the guarantee approximate. Our gated step follows this recipe with an explicit unweighted fallback.\n77:Our contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry \\citep{Aitchison1982Statistical} motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n94:The evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss, drawn from the score families of Section~\\ref{sec:related}: LAC \\citep{Sadinle2019Least} (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS \\citep{Romano2020Classification} (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS \\citep{Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty} (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC (LAC calibrated per label). Exact implementations are those of the evaluator source, not re-derived from the cited papers. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n101:With exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic,Lei2018Distribution}. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration, \\citealt{Vovk2005Algorithmic}); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic} and we do not claim them as new.\n109:If test inputs follow density ratio $w(x)$ with respect to calibration inputs and $w$ is known, placing the test point's weight at $+\\infty$ in the weighted empirical distribution of calibration scores restores marginal coverage \\citep{Tibshirani2019Conformal}; with an estimated $w$ the guarantee is only approximate. Our procedure estimates $w$ with a classifier on summary features, so we treat the weighted branch as a heuristic correction whose benefit has to be measured, not as a guaranteed one.\n/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n42:This paper takes the evaluation protocol as its first-class object. We adopt a single evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families that scores a set-valued predictor on three axes at once (one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio) and that adds negative controls whose expected behaviour is fixed before scoring. Within this protocol we ask:\n51:(iv) We delimit scope: the evidence concerns synthetic families, one sealed evaluation, and one aggregated baseline comparison. We make no claim of a new theorem or of a compositional-geometry method; the procedure operates on summary features of the probability vector.\n128:\\emph{Baselines} are the four evaluator-implemented split-conformal procedures above; only the best baseline score and the paired margin are retained. \\emph{The studied procedure} is Mondrian LAC with gated weighting; it is the configuration frozen by the selection step and evaluated on sealed data. Two nested development configurations bracket it: \\textbf{V1} is Mondrian LAC without weighting, \\textbf{V2} (the studied procedure) adds the gated weighting, and \\textbf{V3} adds, on top of V2, cross-fitted group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds that are activated only by a calibration-only cross-validation gate and otherwise fall back to the V2 path verbatim (also when $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}<300$ or on failure). V1--V3 were scored on development data and are a design comparison, not a significance-tested ablation.\n132:\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development score of V2 and V3 tie exactly, and V2 is the configuration recorded by the selection manifest.\n133:\\paragraph{Sealed final data.} The frozen procedure is evaluated once on sealed cases with seeds 9001--9005, disjoint from the development data by content hash. Each seeded run's score is the mean over cases of $1-\\ell$; the reported final score is the equal-weight mean over seeds and cases. Development and final numbers use different cases and seeds and are never pooled.\n217:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to choose the configuration, so development numbers may be optimistic; this was not measured, and the cause of the development-to-sealed drop is not isolated. The sealed evaluation was run once on disjoint data. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n242:\\caption{Constants of the studied procedure (from the frozen configuration's source).}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"529359a3-cd47-4a89-a29c-a6a024fe0bef","uuid":"714fe193-b719-4654-8e2b-b0b737766f6a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:25:38.449Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"30\n40:Many predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibration set into prediction sets whose marginal coverage is at least $1-\\alpha$ under exchangeability, in finite samples and without distributional assumptions. In practice, however, three questions that matter to a user are not answered by the marginal guarantee: does coverage hold within classes or confidence strata, does it survive a change in the input distribution, and is it achieved with sets that are small enough to be informative? Our motivation is that a method tuned for one question may do worse on another, so reporting only average coverage and set size can hide such differences; conditional-coverage and efficiency work \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Kiyani2024LengthOI} makes the individual questions concrete.\n62:Split conformal prediction uses a held-out calibration set and retains finite-sample marginal coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic,Lei2018Distribution}; Mondrian conformal prediction applies the same rank argument within partition cells \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic}. For classifiers, the least-ambiguous-set construction thresholds the softmax mass of each label \\citep{Sadinle2019Least}, adaptive prediction sets accumulate sorted probability mass with randomisation \\citep{Romano2020Classification}, and a regularised variant penalises large ranks to shrink sets on many-class problems \\citep{Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty}. These are our comparators (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n65:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has been proposed for bias reduction \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-conditional coverage with many classes \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP}, conformal prediction for long-tailed classification \\citep{Ding2025ConformalPF}, and class-wise coverage via augmented label rank calibration \\citep{Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n68:Set size is a second axis: length-optimal conformal prediction \\citep{Kiyani2024LengthOI}, classifier adaptation for efficient sets \\citep{Liu2024CAdapterAD}, the interaction between temperature scaling and conformal sets \\citep{Dabah2024OnTS}, and the quality of foundation-model features as conformal predictors \\citep{Fillioux2024AreFM} all bear on how small a valid set can be. Conformal methods have also been combined with Monte Carlo prediction \\citep{Bethell2023RobustUQ} and interpretable networks \\citep{Huang2024CONFINECP}.\n71:Conformal methods have been developed for time series \\citep{Xu2022ConformalPS}, ordinal labels \\citep{Chakraborty2024DistributionfreeCP}, heteroskedastic regression with conditional validity \\citep{Dewolf2023ConditionalVO}, multivariate residuals \\citep{Braun2025MultivariateSR}, epistemic-uncertainty settings \\citep{Javanmardi2025OptimalCP} and connections to scenario optimisation \\citep{O'Sullivan2025BridgingCP}. Applied treatments of conformal classifiers appear in clinical imaging and histopathology \\citep{Cina2026QuantifyingCC,Gelvez2025ConformalPF}. We describe these works only by title and venue.\n74:Under covariate shift, exchangeability fails. If the density ratio is known, reweighting the calibration scores by it and giving the test point its own weight restores marginal coverage \\citep{Tibshirani2019Conformal}; estimating the ratio with a calibration-versus-test classifier makes the guarantee approximate. Our gated step follows this recipe with an explicit unweighted fallback.\n77:Our contribution is neither a new conformal score nor a new guarantee. It is a combined reading of one evaluation protocol and one composition of established components (Mondrian calibration, likelihood-ratio weighted conformal thresholds, and a held-out gate), with sealed evidence and an explicit account of where it fails. Compositional-data analysis in the sense of log-ratio geometry \\citep{Aitchison1982Statistical} motivates the framing (outputs live on the simplex), but the procedure studied here does not use log-ratio coordinates for its prediction sets.\n94:The evaluator scores four standard split-conformal baselines on identical data with the same loss, drawn from the score families of Section~\\ref{sec:related}: LAC \\citep{Sadinle2019Least} (threshold on $1-p_y$), APS \\citep{Romano2020Classification} (randomised cumulative-mass score), RAPS \\citep{Angelopoulos2020Uncertainty} (APS with a rank penalty, $\\lambda=0.01$, regularisation start $\\min(5,\\max(1,\\lfloor K/5\\rfloor))$) and class-wise LAC (LAC calibrated per label). Exact implementations are those of the evaluator source, not re-derived from the cited papers. Uncalibrated top-mass, full-set and argmax-only sets are implemented as trivial references. The reported baseline score is that of the best of the four baselines under the aggregated score; the margin is $\\score_{\\mathrm{proc}}-\\score_{\\mathrm{best}}$, and its 95\\% interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile of 2000 bootstrap resamples of the per-seed paired differences within each case, averaged with equal weight over cases. The interval therefore reflects seed-to-seed variation inside the evaluated cases and not variation across case designs.\n101:With exchangeable calibration and test points and continuous scores, split conformal with threshold equal to the $k$-th smallest of $n$ calibration scores, $k=\\lceil (n+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, covers with probability $k/(n+1)\\in[1-\\alpha,\\,1-\\alpha+1/(n+1))$, and coverage conditional on the calibration set follows a $\\mathrm{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$ law \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic,Lei2018Distribution}. Applying the same rank argument within each cell of a partition $G(x,y)$ yields cell-conditional coverage (Mondrian calibration, \\citealt{Vovk2005Algorithmic}); with $G$ equal to the label this is class-conditional coverage, and with $G$ a function of $x$ alone it is conditional on the stratum. These are textbook statements \\citep{Vovk2005Algorithmic} and we do not claim them as new.\n109:If test inputs follow density ratio $w(x)$ with respect to calibration inputs and $w$ is known, placing the test point's weight at $+\\infty$ in the weighted empirical distribution of calibration scores restores marginal coverage \\citep{Tibshirani2019Conformal}; with an estimated $w$ the guarantee is only approximate. Our procedure estimates $w$ with a classifier on summary features, so we treat the weighted branch as a heuristic correction whose benefit has to be measured, not as a guaranteed one.\n/workspace/paper/.claude/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n42:This paper takes the evaluation protocol as its first-class object. We adopt a single evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families that scores a set-valued predictor on three axes at once (one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio) and that adds negative controls whose expected behaviour is fixed before scoring. Within this protocol we ask:\n51:(iv) We delimit scope: the evidence concerns synthetic families, one sealed evaluation, and one aggregated baseline comparison. We make no claim of a new theorem or of a compositional-geometry method; the procedure operates on summary features of the probability vector.\n128:\\emph{Baselines} are the four evaluator-implemented split-conformal procedures above; only the best baseline score and the paired margin are retained. \\emph{The studied procedure} is Mondrian LAC with gated weighting; it is the configuration frozen by the selection step and evaluated on sealed data. Two nested development configurations bracket it: \\textbf{V1} is Mondrian LAC without weighting, \\textbf{V2} (the studied procedure) adds the gated weighting, and \\textbf{V3} adds, on top of V2, cross-fitted group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds that are activated only by a calibration-only cross-validation gate and otherwise fall back to the V2 path verbatim (also when $n_{\\mathrm{cal}}<300$ or on failure). V1--V3 were scored on development data and are a design comparison, not a significance-tested ablation.\n132:\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development score of V2 and V3 tie exactly, and V2 is the configuration recorded by the selection manifest.\n133:\\paragraph{Sealed final data.} The frozen procedure is evaluated once on sealed cases with seeds 9001--9005, disjoint from the development data by content hash. Each seeded run's score is the mean over cases of $1-\\ell$; the reported final score is the equal-weight mean over seeds and cases. Development and final numbers use different cases and seeds and are never pooled.\n217:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to choose the configuration, so development numbers may be optimistic; this was not measured, and the cause of the development-to-sealed drop is not isolated. The sealed evaluation was run once on disjoint data. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n242:\\caption{Constants of the studied procedure (from the frozen configuration's source).}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
